From 5bd7f3ce3b42d60806d2b698c6347d1a242be522 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 10:00:41 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 08/10] ppc64le: the arch name bun calls itself, and the
 registry fixtures' cpu lists
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three places still spelled the host architecture by hand.

harness gains `bunArch` — the name bun uses for itself in release assets, in
what `bun upgrade` looks for, and in `bun build --compile` targets. It is not
process.arch: bun spells arm64 "aarch64" and ppc64 "ppc64le". bun-upgrade's
local copy of that map is gone, and bun-build-compile's "compile with current
platform target string" uses it instead of `isArm64 ? "aarch64" : "x64"` --
which on any third architecture asked for a cross target and failed trying to
download bun-linux-x64.

The Verdaccio fixtures for the "native package for this host" cases --
test-postinstall-skip-native, test-native-binlink-target,
test-native-binlink-altpath-target, test-native-binlink-fallback-target --
declared `cpu: ["arm64", "x64"]`, so on ppc64le they were disabled for the
host: bun-prune's "plain prune keeps them" had nothing to keep (4 failures),
and bun-install-native-binlink kept falling back to the non-native bin (12
failures). They now list every architecture bun runs on. The deliberately
never-matching fixtures (native-foo-x64 and friends, whose `os` is "foo") are
untouched.
